pan></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop op-limits\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.05em;\"><span style=\"top:-1.8723em;margin-left:0em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span><span class=\"mop op-symbol large-op\">∑</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.2777em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.453em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.247em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:2.7204em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div><h2 id=\"algorithm\">Algorithm</h2><p>Accumulate screened real-space pairs and Gaussian-damped reciprocal modes, then subtract the self energy. Restore the same Coulomb prefactor to every term in dimensional units. Changing α redistributes work between sums but cannot change their converged total. Derive forces from the same energy convention.</p><h2 id=\"conditions\">Conditions and pitfalls</h2><p>A net-charged cell requires an additional convention such as a compensating background. Specify potential gauge, surface term, periodic self images and molecular exclusions separately. A periodic gravitational Poisson solve changes the kernel/sign and mean-density treatment; it is not the same calculation.</p><p>The companion release keeps an asymmetric neutral eight-charge cubic cell, triply periodic with tin-foil boundaries. It separates the direct-Ewald splitting parameter, real-image and reciprocal-cube cutoffs, using a separately implemented heat-kernel theta integral as a reference. It does not extend to charged backgrounds or slabs.</p><h2 id=\"verification\">What to check</h2><p>For a small neutral configuration, increase both cutoffs independently and test α independence. Compare forces with finite differences of energy, and check translation invariance and net force. Agreement between two unconverged sums is not independent validation.</p><p>Historical and companion inputs, criteria and results are retained separately. The companion checks energy and every particle force, refinement of the reference integral itself, translations, particle ordering, net force and energy differences individually; a deliberately truncated control is distinct from a converged reference.</p><h2 id=\"scoped-examples\">Check fixed inputs: separate scoped releases</h2><p>Check each example’s inputs, observables, diagnostic definitions and scope. What a passing check establishes depends on its acceptance criteria and differs from a general mathematical guarantee. Historical inputs, measurements and criteria are preserved.</p><article class=\"resource-summary\" id=\"supplement-ewald\"><h3>Neutral-cell Ewald sums and an independent theta integral</h3><p>Computation for a fixed target</p><p>Implement real-space, reciprocal-space and self Ewald terms for neutral 3D periodic Coulomb charges; vary splitting and cutoffs.</p><p>No charged-cell/background correction, triclinic cell, finite-dielectric surface term, slab correction, stress or material-specific force field.</p><p><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/validation/ewald/?lang=en\">Input, results and acceptance</a> · <a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/en/read/validation-particles/#ewald\">Method, equations and sources</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/run.py\">Code: run.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/electrostatics.py\">Code: electrostatics.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/fmm.py\">Code: fmm.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/transport.py\">Code: transport.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/test_particles.py\">Tests: test_particles.py</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/atlas-particles-2026-11-22.zip\">Release ZIP</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/results/result.json\">Saved results</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particles/verification.json\">Independent-extraction rerun receipt</a></p></article><article class=\"resource-summary\" id=\"supplement-ewald-convergence\"><h3>Ewald: asymmetric-cell energies and forces versus independent theta integration</h3><p>Computation for a fixed target</p><p>Compare a neutral eight-charge cell energy and all 24 force components with a separately coded Gaussian/theta integral. Separate splitting and real/reciprocal truncation from invariants and conservation.</p><p>One fixed neutral cubic cell. Upper-limit sensitivity is not a rigorous tail bound for arbitrary configurations. The theta reference is an independently formulated original implementation, not an external package or published numerical table. Charged/triclinic/slab cells, stress and long MD are outside scope.</p><p><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/validation/ewald-convergence/?lang=en\">Input, results and acceptance</a> · <a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/en/read/validation-particle-transport/#ewald-convergence\">Method, equations and sources</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/run.py\">Code: run.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/longrange_experiments.py\">Code: longrange_experiments.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/electrostatics.py\">Code: electrostatics.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/fmm.py\">Code: fmm.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/kinetic_experiments.py\">Code: kinetic_experiments.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/kinetics.py\">Code: kinetics.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/test_kinetics.py\">Tests: test_kinetics.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/test_longrange.py\">Tests: test_longrange.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/test_runner.py\">Tests: test_runner.py</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/atlas-particle-transport-2026-11-22.zip\">Release ZIP</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/results/result.json\">Saved results</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-22/particle-transport/verification.json\">Independent-extraction rerun receipt</a></p></article><h2 id=\"publication-scope\">Publication scope and related entries</h2><p>The historical reading, equations, sources and release are preserved; a separate companion release supplies bounded checks for fixed conditions. This does not mean every general verification suggestion above has been executed. Consult each release’s dedicated page and saved record for inputs, references, checks and limits.</p><ul><li><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/methods/ewald/?lang=en\">Ewald summation</a></li></ul><h2 id=\"sources\">Primary publications, supporting sources and review scope</h2><p>Distinguish original research, later variants, author monographs, pedagogical articles and official references. Bibliographic verification differs from consulting selected passages; neither implies a full-paper audit or numerical reproduction. Access-depth notes accompany each source.</p><ol class=\"reading-sources\"><li id=\"source-ewald1921\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/andp.19213690304\">Ewald, P. P. (1921). Die Berechnung optischer und elektrostatischer Gitterpotentiale</a>. Annalen der Physik<p>Research publication: proposal or related formulation · Bibliography checked · <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/andp.19213690304\">Consulted record</a></p><p>Reviewed as a source for priority-method metadata. The individual method records distinguish reference roles, relevant full-text passages, abstracts and bibliographic checks; this does not imply a new numerical validation.</p></li><li id=\"source-hockney1981book\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1201/9780367806934\">Hockney, R. W. and Eastwood, J. W. (1981). Computer Simulation Using Particles</a>. McGraw-Hill<p>Author monograph / supporting reference · Bibliography checked · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1201/9780367806934\">Consulted record</a></p><p>Original book date is 1981, as cited by Barnes and Hut (1986); DOI identifies a later Taylor &amp; Francis reissue. Reference for particle-mesh methods and isolated doubled-domain convolution; the self-cell kernel convention is stated separately in our code.</p></li></ol>",
